<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tadeusz Kościuszko</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1746">1746</a>–<a href="/years/1817">1817</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tadeusz Kościuszko was a Polish-Lithuanian military engineer and commander who also served in the American War of Independence. In 1794 he led a national insurrection against Russian and Prussian domination of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>. The uprising's defeat was followed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-partition-of-poland">Third Partition</a> of 1795, which ended the Commonwealth's existence, and Kościuszko became an enduring symbol of Polish national resistance.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tadeusz-kosciuszko.jpg" alt="Tadeusz Kościuszko" width="317" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Karl Gottlieb Schweikart · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karl_G_Schweikart_-_Tadeusz_Ko%C5%9Bciuszko_(%C3%96aL).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tadeusz Kościuszko was born in February 1746 on the Mereczowszczyzna estate in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>, now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a>; his exact birthdate is unknown. He was the youngest son of Ludwik Tadeusz Kościuszko, an officer and member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szlachta">szlachta</a>, whose family held modest lands worked by 31 peasant families. After graduating from the Corps of Cadets in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1769 on a royal scholarship. As a foreigner he could not enter the French military academies, so he enrolled in the Royal Academy of Painting and Sculpture, studied architecture privately, and audited military lectures for five years. The French Enlightenment and the economic theory of physiocracy left a strong impression on him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1776 Kościuszko sailed for America and, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-franklin">Benjamin Franklin</a>'s recommendation, was commissioned a colonel of engineers in the Continental Army. He built fortifications including those at West Point, and as chief engineer to General Nathanael Greene he helped the Continental Army cross the rivers in the Race to the Dan in 1781. In 1783 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/continental-congress">Continental Congress</a> promoted him to brigadier general.</p><p>He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1784 and became a major general in the Commonwealth army in 1789. In the Polish-Russian War of 1792 he commanded a division, and although he lost no battle, the king capitulated and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-partition-of-poland">Second Partition</a> followed. In March 1794, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>'s Main Square, he announced an uprising and took the title Naczelnik. He won at Racławice on 4 April by leading a charge of peasant volunteers armed with scythes, and at Połaniec he issued a proclamation granting serfs civil rights and reducing their labour obligations. He was captured at the Battle of Maciejowice in October 1794, and the uprising's defeat in November led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-partition-of-poland">Third Partition</a> of 1795.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine II</a> died, Tsar Paul I pardoned Kościuszko in 1796 and he left for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. A friend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a>, he wrote a will in 1798 dedicating his American assets to the education and freedom of enslaved people in the United States, but its execution proved difficult and the funds were never used as he intended. He eventually returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> until his death on 15 October 1817. He is a national hero in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, the United States, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tadeusz Kościuszko” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The communist authorities after the war also kept the song, making it the anthem of the 1st <b>Tadeusz Kościuszko</b> Infantry Division.</blockquote><a href="/songs/rota">Rota</a></li><li><blockquote>In Kraków, <b>Tadeusz Kościuszko</b> proclaimed a general uprising against Russia and Prussia and took command of all Polish forces.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-24">24 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Mieračoŭščyna, in what is now <a href="/countries/belarus">Belarus</a>. Died in Solothurn, in what is now <a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.77,25.12&amp;year=1792" aria-label="Mieračoŭščyna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5112 345 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Tadeusz Kościuszko"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5562 615h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5562 615h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.77,25.12&amp;year=1792">What was here in 1792? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
